÷1÷The_Open_Watcom_Browser÷The Open Watcom Browser÷The Open Watcom Browser
îThe_Open_Watcom_Browser

ìBrowserì Imagine being assigned to a project with thousands of lines of C++ source code.  Learning the relationship between the objects of such an application can be very difficult.  The Browser was developed to solve this very problem - to help developers better understand the source code they are working with.  Information such as the class inheritance hierarchy and the call tree for an application can be studied while using the Browser.  Once you have located a symbol, you can quickly view the source file that contains its definition or list all the files that reference it.  Viewing these relationships helps you understand how the program works.

This chapter describes the many tasks you can perform with the Browser, including creating and opening a Browser database file, configuring the Browser session, and viewing the list of all symbols in your program, the class inheritance hierarchy, and call structure.
´
÷2÷Using_the_Browser÷Using the Browser÷The Open Watcom Browser
îUsing_the_Browser

This section discusses the following topics:

Òc
Ó Starting the Browser

Ó Starting the Browser through the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment

Ó Quitting the Browser

Ó The Browser Menu Bar
Ô
´
÷3÷Starting_the_Browser÷Starting the Browser÷The Open Watcom Browser
îStarting_the_Browser

ìBrowser, startì ìstart, Browserì To start the Browser, double click on the Browser icon.  This opens the Browser window.  The caption bar of this window displays the current option file and Browser database file.  The messagecourier12 no browser filehelv10 appears on this line if a database file is not currently selected.

The Browser is an integrated tool that you can open from the IDE so that you can browse the source code of the project you are working on.  Refer to the IDE guide for further information on the Integrated Development Environment.
´
÷3÷Quitting_the_Browser÷Quitting the Browser÷The Open Watcom Browser
îQuitting_the_Browser

ìBrowser, leaveì ìleave, Browserì To exit the Browser, chooseïbï Exitğ from theïbï Fileğ menu of the Browser window.  If you made changes to the options during the Browser session, a message box appears prompting you to save the changes.

Chooseïbï Noğ in the message box to close the Browser session without saving.  Any options changed within the session are lost.

Chooseïbï Yesğ in the message box to save the current options to the current option file, if one exists, and exit the Browser session.  If no option file exists, aïbï Save Asğ dialog appears that allows you to specify the option file to which you want to save the new options.

Ê
Ì 
¹ïbï Selectğïbï Cancelğ to return to the main Browser window without saving the options.

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Selectğïbï OKğ to close the dialog and exit the Browser session.
Ë
´
÷3÷The_Browser_Menu_Bar÷The Browser Menu Bar÷The Open Watcom Browser
îThe_Browser_Menu_Bar

ìBrowser, Menu Barì The Browser's menu bar consists of the following eight menus:

Ê
ÌFile
¹ Create, open, and configure Browser files; save and load options

ÌView
¹ Choose a global view for the current database file

ÌDetail
¹ View detailed information for a symbol

ÌTree
¹ Choose the nodes to display for a tree view

ÌLocate
¹ Find symbols within the current global view

ÌOptions
¹ Configure the Browser session

ÌWindows
¹ Select from the list of Browser windows currently open

ÌHelp
¹ Access on-line help information
Ë
´
÷2÷Browser_Files÷Browser Files÷The Open Watcom Browser
îBrowser_Files

ìBrowser, filesì ìmodule file, in Browserì To browse your source code, you must first create a Browser module file for each source file that you wish to browse.  Currently, browsing is supported by the Open Watcom C/C++ compilers only (Open Watcom FORTRAN 77 does not support browsing).  To create a Browser module file, specify the "db" option when you compile the source file.  If you are using the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment, selectïbï Emit Browser Informationğ from theïbï Debugging Switchesğ panel of theïbï Compiler Switchesğ dialog.  The Browser module file will have the same name as the source file and an extension of ".MBR".

Once the Browser module files have been created, these files are merged and a Browser database file is created.  This process eliminates redundant information such as duplicate definitions that occur when a header file is included by many source files.  Browser database files have the extension ".DBR".

The Browser allows you to configure your session and saves this configuration to an options file.  Options files have the extension ".OBR".  The file namecourier12 setup.obrhelv10 is the default name of the options file.  The default options file is automatically loaded by the Browser when the Browser is started.
´
÷3÷Creating_a_Browser_Database_File_within_the_Browser÷Creating a Browser Database File within the Browser÷The Open Watcom Browser
îCreating_a_Browser_Database_File_within_the_Browser

ìBrowser, create database fileì ìdatabase file, create in Browserì

Ê
Ì(1)
¹ïbï Chooseğïbï Newğ from theïbï Fileğ menu.

This opens theïbï New Browser Fileğ dialog where you enter the name of the Browser database file you are creating.

courier12°c°brow1.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 1.ğïibïğïbï Use the New Browser File dialog to create a new Browser database file.ğ

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Type the name and pathğ of the new Browser database file.

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Click on theğïbï Filesğ button to open a second dialog that allows you to browse the directory structure for an existing Browser database file.  Select an existing file and change its name.  If you do not change its name, a message box appears when you pressïbï OKğ asking if you want to overwrite the existing file.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Click onğïbï OK.ğ

This opens aïbï Moduleğ window that will eventually contain a list of the module files that will make up the database file.

Ì(4)
¹ïbï Click on theğïbï Addğ button in theïbï Moduleğ window.

This opens theïbï Select Module File(s)ğ dialog where you choose the module files to add to the database file.

Ì(5)
¹ïbï Select the module filesğ to add to the database file and click onïbï OK.ğ

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Double clickğ on the desired module file.

This closes theïbï Select Module File(s)ğ dialog and adds the selected module file to the Browser file component list.  Each module file on the components list has a check box.  When added, this check box is marked with an X, indicating that the module file is enabled.

Ì(6)
¹ïbï Continue with steps 4 and 5ğ until you have added all desired module files to the component list.

courier12°c°brow2.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 2.ğïibïğïbï The Modules dialog displays the selected module files.ğ

Ì(7)
¹ïbï Click onğïbï OKğ on theïbï Moduleğ window.

This closes theïbï Moduleğ window and creates the database file.
Ë
