÷2÷Global_Views÷Global Views÷The Open Watcom Browser
îGlobal_Views

The menu items under the View menu let you display a global view of your program.  A global view is one that displays relationships between all symbols in your program.  The following are global views.

Ê
ÌList
¹ Displays a list of all symbols in your program

ÌInheritance
¹ Displays the class inheritance graph for your program

ÌCall
¹ Displays the call graph for your program
Ë

Once a global view has been displayed, you can view detailed information for the symbols in the global view.  Refer to the section entitled Viewing_Detail_InformationViewing Detail Information for a discussion on displaying detail information.

It is possible to specify a query that restricts the symbols displayed in global views.  See the section entitled Global_Symbol_QueriesGlobal Symbol Queries for more information.
´
÷3÷The_List_View÷The List View÷The Open Watcom Browser
îThe_List_View

ìBrowser, browsing symbolsì ìsymbols, browsingì Using the Browser to view the symbols in your program is much faster than searching through your source code for symbol information.  From the symbols list you can quickly access detailed information on a symbol that tells you where the symbol is used and where it is defined.

To view a list of all symbols in the current Browser database file, chooseïbï Listğ from theïbï Viewğ menu.  This displays a window which can list all symbols in your program.  The window has a vertical scroll bar that allows you to scroll through the list of symbols in your program.  Since your program may contain a very large number of symbols, the Browser does not load all the symbols in your program from the database.  Instead, only the number of symbols that can be displayed in the window are loaded.  As far as the Browser is concerned, this list is infinite.  For this reason, the scroll thumb on the vertical scroll bar is positioned in the middle of the vertical scroll bar and cannot be moved.  Click below the vertical scroll thumb to view the next page of symbols and above the vertical scroll thumb to view the previous page of symbols.

courier12°c°brow4.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 4.ğïibïğïbï The List window displays all symbols in the current browser database file.ğ

Each symbol has a icon to its left.  A letter marking each icon indicates the symbol type in the source code as follows:

Ê
ÌF
¹ Function

ÌC
¹ Class

ÌT
¹ Typedef

ÌV
¹ Variable

ÌE
¹ Enum
Ë

There are several ways to display the detail view of symbols.  When you reveal the detail view, the file folder icon changes to an open file folder.  To close the detail view dialog, click on the file folder.  This changes the icon back to a closed folder and closes the dialog.  To reveal the detail view of a symbol you can perform any of the following actions:

Òc
Ó The file folder icons are hot spots that display the detail view of the selected symbol.  To activate the hot spot, click once on the symbol name to select it and press ENTER to reveal the detail view.

Ó Click once on the file folder to reveal the detail view for that symbol.

Ó Click once on the symbol name to select it and chooseïbï Detailğ from theïbï Detailğ menu.

Ó Double click on the symbol name.
Ô
´
÷3÷The_Class_Inheritance_View÷The Class Inheritance View÷The Open Watcom Browser
îThe_Class_Inheritance_View

ìBrowser, browsing classesì ìbrowsing classesì ìinheritance menu itemì Selectingïbï Inheritanceğ from theïbï Viewğ menu displays the inheritance hierarchy of all of the C++ classes in your program using a tree.  This allows you to see the relationships between base classes and derived classes.  In the inheritance hierarchy, each node represents a class.

courier12°c°brow5.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 5.ğïibïğïbï The Inheritance view displays the hierarchy of all C++ classes in your program (graph view shown).ğ

There are two different tree views you can choose from to display the class hierarchy:

Ê
ÌGraph view
¹ The graph view displays each class in a box.  A box, or node, is connected to another node if one is a derived class of the other.  The root node of the tree is the base class for all nodes in the tree.  The tree is initially fully expanded.  See the section entitled Manipulating_the_Tree_ViewsManipulating the Tree Views for more information on removing and expanding nodes from the tree.

ÌOutline view
¹ The outline view displays the same information as the graph view but in a different way.  The outline view is initially fully collapsed.  That is, only the base classes are displayed.  In order to view the derived classes of a node, you must single click on the node.  This expands the node, displaying all its immediate derived classes.  See the section entitled Manipulating_the_Tree_ViewsManipulating the Tree Views for more information on removing and expanding nodes from the tree.
Ë
´
÷3÷The_Function_Call_Tree_View÷The Function Call Tree View÷The Open Watcom Browser
îThe_Function_Call_Tree_View

ìBrowser, browsing functionsì ìbrowsing functionsì ìcall menu itemì Selectingïbï Callğ from theïbï Viewğ menu displays the function call tree for all functions in your program.  This allows you to see all the functions that a given function calls and conversely all functions that call a certain function.  In the call tree, each node represents a function.

courier12°c°brow6.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 6.ğïibïğïbï The Call view displays a call tree for all functions in your program (outline view shown).ğ

There are two different tree views you can choose from to display the function call tree:

Ê
ÌGraph view
¹ The graph view displays each function in a box.  A box, or node, is connected to another node if one function calls the other.  The tree is initially fully expanded.  See the section entitled Manipulating_the_Tree_ViewsManipulating the Tree Views for more information on removing and expanding nodes from the tree.

ÌOutline view
¹ The outline view displays the same information as the graph view but in a different way.  The outline view is initially fully collapsed.  In order to view the functions called by the function specified in the node, you must single click on the node.  This expands the node, displaying all functions it calls.  See the section entitled Manipulating_the_Tree_ViewsManipulating the Tree Views for more information on removing and expanding nodes from the tree.
Ë
´
÷3÷Manipulating_the_Tree_Views÷Manipulating the Tree Views÷The Open Watcom Browser
îManipulating_the_Tree_Views

ìBrowser, graph viewì ìBrowser, outline viewì With both the graph view and the outline view you can change the information displayed in the following ways:

Òc
Ó select the root nodes you want to view

Ó expand any node in the view

Ó collapse any node in the view
Ô

You expand and collapse nodes in the graph and outline views to hide and reveal descendant and ancestor nodes of the selected node.  There are two ways to expand and collapse nodes:

Òc
Ó choose the desired function from theïbï Treeğ menu

Ó click on the node (applies only to the outline view)
Ô

In addition to these functions, you can force the graph to be redrawn when a node in the tree view is collapsed.  By default, collapsed nodes will leave a gap in the graph.  Redrawing the graph removes these gaps.  This feature is controlled by an option.  See the section entitled Configuring_the_BrowserConfiguring the Browser for more information.

Clicking on the right mouse button when the mouse cursor is in a tree view will automatically display theïbï Treeğ menu.  This allows you to perform the actions in theïbï Treeğ menu without actually going to the menu bar.
´
÷4÷Selecting_Root_Nodes÷Selecting Root Nodes÷The Open Watcom Browser
îSelecting_Root_Nodes

ìRoot Nodesì ìNodes, rootì ìBrowser, selecting root nodesì Selectïbï Select Root Nodesğ from theïbï Treeğ menu to change the root nodes that are displayed.  The root nodes you select appear in the graph or outline view; all other root nodes are hidden.

Choosingïbï Select Root Nodesğ from theïbï Treeğ menu opens theïbï Select Root Nodesğ dialog.  This dialog lists all of the symbols that appear as a root node.  Each symbol in theïbï Select Root Nodesğ dialog has a check box.  When enabled, this check box is marked with an X, indicating that the symbol will appear as a root node in the display.

courier12°c°brow7.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 7.ğïibïğïbï On the Select Root Nodes dialog, choose the symbols you want to appear as root nodes.ğ

helv12ïbïDisabling Root Nodesğhelv10
ìRoot Nodes, disableì ìBrowser, disable root nodesì

Ê
Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click in the check boxğ of the root node you want to disable.  Alternatively, use the up and down arrow keys to select the root node you wish to disable.  Press the space bar to disable the currently selected root node.

The X disappears meaning that the root node is disabled and will not appear in the display.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Repeat step oneğ until you have disabled all desired root nodes.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Click onğïbï OKğ in theïbï Select Root Nodesğ dialog.

Theïbï Select Root Nodesğ dialog closes and the display is updated.
Ë

To disable all of the root nodes in theïbï Select Root Nodesğ dialog, click on theïbï Disable Allğ button.  This removes Xs from each box in theïbï Select Root Nodesğ dialog.  Click onïbï OKğ to close the dialog and update the display.

Ê
ÌNote:
¹ This disables the selected root nodes only for the active view window.
Ë

helv12ïbïEnabling Root Nodesğhelv10
ìBrowser, enable root nodesì ìRoot Nodes, enableì

Ê
Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click in the check boxğ of the root node you want to enable.  Alternatively, use the up and down arrow keys to select the root node you wish to enable.  Press the space bar to enable the currently selected root node.

An X appears in the box indicating that the root node is enabled and will appear in the display.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Repeat step oneğ until you have enabled all desired root nodes.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Click onğïbï OKğ in theïbï Select Root Nodesğ dialog.

Theïbï Select Root Nodesğ dialog closes and the display is updated.
Ë

To enable all of the root nodes in theïbï Select Root Nodesğ dialog, click on theïbï Enable Allğ button.  An X appears in each box on theïbï Select Root Nodesğ dialog.  Click onïbï OKğ to close the dialog and update the display.

Ê
ÌNote:
¹ This enables the selected root nodes only for the active view window.
Ë
´
÷4÷Expanding_Nodes÷Expanding Nodes÷The Open Watcom Browser
îExpanding_Nodes

ìNodes, expandingì ìBrowser, expanding nodesì You can expand the display all at once, one level at a time, or one branch at a time.  To expand by levels and branches, you must first select the node you want to expand.  Do this by clicking once on the desired node.

Ê
ÌExpand One Level
¹ Chooseïbï Expand One Levelğ from theïbï Treeğ menu to display all of the immediate children for the selected node.

ÌExpand Branch
¹ Chooseïbï Expand Branchğ from theïbï Treeğ menu to display all descendants of the selected node.

ÌExpand All
¹ Chooseïbï Expand Allğ from theïbï Treeğ menu to fully expand all of the enabled root nodes.  Disabled root nodes do not appear in the display.
Ë
´
÷4÷Collapsing_Nodes÷Collapsing Nodes÷The Open Watcom Browser
îCollapsing_Nodes

ìNodes, collapsingì ìBrowser, collapsing nodesì You can collapse the display all at once or one branch at a time.  To collapse by branches, you must first select the node you want to collapse.  Do this by clicking once on the desired node.

Ê
ÌCollapse Branch
¹ Chooseïbï Collapse Branchğ from theïbï Treeğ menu to hide all descendants of the selected node.

ÌCollapse All
¹ Chooseïbï Collapse Allğ from theïbï Treeğ menu to collapse all of the nodes and display only the root nodes.
Ë

helv12ïbïUsing the Keyboardğhelv10
ìexpanding and collapsing nodes, using the keyboardì ìusing the keyboard, to expand and collapse nodesì You can expand and collapse the symbols using key sequences.  Click once on the symbol name to highlight it and press one of the following keys:

Ê
Ì+
¹ Expand the symbol one level

Ì-
¹ Collapse the symbol one level

Ì*
¹ Expand all descendants of the selected node.  This is the same as choosingïbï Expand Branchğ from theïbï Treeğ menu.

ÌCTRL *
¹ Expands all of the enabled root nodes.  This is the same as choosingïbï Expand Allğ from theïbï Treeğ menu.

ÌCTRL -
¹ Collapses all of the symbols and displays only the root nodes.  This is the same as choosingïbï Collapse Allğ from theïbï Treeğ menu.
Ë
´
÷2÷Viewing_Detail_Information÷Viewing Detail Information÷The Open Watcom Browser
îViewing_Detail_Information

ìBrowser, view detail informationì Once a global view is displayed, you can view detailed information for a symbol in the global view in several ways:

Òc
Ó Double click on the desired symbol.

Ó Click once on the desired symbol to select it; then chooseïbï Detailğ from theïbï Detailğ menu.

Ó Click once on the desired symbol to select it then press ENTER.
Ô

From the list view, you can reveal the detail view if you:

Òc
Ó Click once on the icon to the left of the symbol.
Ô

Performing any of these actions reveals a detail view window for the selected symbol.  The same information appears in this window regardless of the method used to access it.

courier12°c°brow8.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 8.ğïibïğïbï The detail view displays detailed information for the selected symbol.ğ

The detail view window displays the source file where the symbol is defined or declared and it shows you what the symbol looks like in your source code.

A list box appears in the detail view window when you select a symbol whose type is a function, class, or enum.  The list box contains information specific to the symbol type, as follows.

Ê
ÌFunctions
¹ The list box displays, where applicable, local variables for the function.

ÌClasses
¹ The list box displays member variables and member functions for the selected class and any inherited classes.  The Browser divides this information into three categories:

Òc
Ó Public

Ó Private

Ó Protected
Ô

ÌEnums
¹ The list box displays enumerator values for the selected enumerator.
Ë

You can view detailed information for symbols in the list box by double-clicking on the symbol.  Other symbols in the header information for the detail view are highlighted.  Double-clicking on these symbols also displays a detail view.
´
÷3÷Goto_Definition÷Goto Definition÷The Open Watcom Browser
îGoto_Definition

ìBrowser, Goto Definition functionì ìGoto Definition functionì From a detail view,ïbï Goto Definitionğ in theïbï Detailğ menu allows you to edit the file that contains the symbol's definition.  The editor is positioned on the line and column containing the symbols definition.  This allows you to make edits to your source code while you are browsing.  When you are done, save any changes made and exit the editor to return to the Browser session.

Note that any changes to your source code will make the Browser database out-of-date.
´
÷3÷Source_References÷Source References÷The Open Watcom Browser
îSource_References

ìBrowser, source referencesì ìSource Referencesì Selectingïbï Source Referencesğ from theïbï Detailğ menu displays all locations in the source code where a symbol is referenced.  This allows you to analyze all uses of a particular symbol.

When modifying a symbol, you can use this feature to locate all occurrences of the symbol in the source code so you can update them.

Ê
ÌTo view the source references for a symbol:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Position yourself at the detail view of the symbolğ whose source references you want to view.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Chooseğïbï Source Referencesğ from theïbï Detailğ menu.

This opens theïbï Source Referencesğ window for the selected symbol.  This window displays the filename, line number, and column number of each occurrence of the selected symbol in the source code.
Ë

courier12°c°brow9.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 9.ğïibïğïbï Use the Source References window to view all the occurrences of the selected symbol in the source code.ğ
´
÷3÷Symbol_References÷Symbol References÷The Open Watcom Browser
îSymbol_References

ìSymbol referencesì ìBrowser, symbol referencesìïbï Symbol Referencesğ allows you to view a list of all symbols that use a particular symbol.  When modifying a symbol you can use this feature to locate all symbols using the modified symbol.  This allows you to determine if all referencing symbols need to be updated.

Ê
ÌTo view the symbol references for a symbol:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Position yourself at the detail view of the symbolğ whose symbol references you want to view.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Chooseğïbï Symbol Referencesğ from theïbï Detailğ menu.

This opens theïbï Symbol Referencingğ window for the selected symbol.  This window displays a list of all symbols that use the selected symbol.  The display is the same as the list view of symbols.  Each symbol has a icon indicating its symbol type.

courier12°c°brow10.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 10.ğïibïğïbï Use the Symbols window to view a list of all symbols that use the selected symbol.ğ

You can perform the same actions in this window as from theïbï Listğ window.  Refer to the section entitled The_List_ViewThe List View for more information.
Ë

From this window you can invoke the editor to make changes to the source code that contains the symbols that reference a particular symbol.  Select the reference you want to edit in one of two ways:

Òc
Ó Double click on the desired reference.

Ó Click once on the desired reference to select it and press ENTER.
Ô

This invokes the editor and positions the cursor at the line that contains the selected reference.
´
÷2÷Locating_Symbols÷Locating Symbols÷The Open Watcom Browser
îLocating_Symbols

ìSymbols, locatingì ìBrowser, locating symbolsì When a global view is displayed and your program contains many symbols, it can be difficult to scroll through the global view to locate a particular symbol.  There are two functions that can help you locate a symbol in a global view:

Òc
Ó Find

Ó Find Selected
Ô
´
÷3÷Find÷Find÷The Open Watcom Browser
îFind

Chooseïbï Findğ from theïbï Locateğ menu to open theïbï Findğ dialog.  Use this dialog to specify the criteria to be used to locate a symbol.  For example, you can search for a particular symbol or a set of symbols with common characteristics.

courier12°c°brow11.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 11.ğïibïğïbï Use the Find dialog to locate one symbol or a set of symbols.ğ
´
÷4÷Find_Pattern÷Find Pattern÷The Open Watcom Browser
îFind_Pattern

ìBrowser, find patternì ìfind patternì In theïbï Patternğ field of theïbï Findğ dialog, enter a pattern for the symbol(s) you wish to locate.  The matching behaviour depends on the switches set in this dialog.

Ê
ÌMatch Case
¹ When enabled, the Browser performs a case sensitive compare when attempting to find a match.

ÌMatch Whole Symbols Only
¹ When enabled, the Browser locates symbols that exactly match the specified pattern.  This switch only applies when theïbï Use Regular Expressionsğ switch is disabled.

ÌUse Regular Expressions
¹ When enabled, the Browser interprets the pattern specified as a regular expression.  Theïbï Editğ button, when pressed, displays theïbï Regular Expression Optionsğ dialog.  This dialog allows you to specify the regular expression.
Ë

Dropping the combo box of theïbï Patternğ field displays a list of previous patterns entered.  You can select a pattern from this list instead of entering a new one.
´
÷4÷Find_Filters÷Find Filters÷The Open Watcom Browser
îFind_Filters

ìfind filtersì ìBrowser, find filtersì Clicking on theïbï Filtersğ button on theïbï Findğ dialog opens theïbï Find Filtersğ dialog.  This dialog allows you to specify the characteristics of the symbol(s) to be located.

courier12°c°brow12.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 12.ğïibïğïbï Use the Find Filters dialog to specify characteristics of the symbol(s) you want to locate.ğ

In theïbï Symbol Typeğ section of the dialog click on the symbol types you want to view.  To search for all symbol types, select theïbï Allğ button.

In theïbï Symbol Scopeğ section, you can specify the scope for locating symbols.

Ê
ÌMember of Class:
¹ Specify the class that the symbol must be a member of in order to be located.

ÌLocal Symbols of Function:
¹ Specify the function that the symbol must be local to in order to be located.
Ë

helv12ïbïFind File Filtersğhelv10
Theïbï Source Filesğ button, when pressed, displays theïbï Source Filesğ dialog that allows you to specify the files to be searched when attempting to locate a symbol.  This dialog lists all source files that make up the database file.  Each source file in the list has a check box.  An X in this box indicates that the source file is searched when trying to locate a symbol.  Click in the check box of the source file you want to disable.  This removes the X.  The blank box indicates that the source file will not be searched when trying to locate a symbol.  To re-enable the source file, click in the check box again.  This places an X in the box indicating that the source file will be searched.

courier12°c°brow13.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 13.ğïibïğïbï Choose the source files to be searched using the Source Files dialog.ğ

Theïbï Patternğ section of the dialog, allows you to specify wild card directory specifications for files that are to be included or excluded in the search.  For example, specifying "d:\watcom\h\*.h" and pressing theïbï Excludeğ button, will prevent any file in the "d:\watcom\h" directory with extension ".h" from being searched.  To include these files in the search again, specify the same pattern and press theïbï Includeğ button.

Pressing theïbï Set Allğ button includes all files in the search.

Pressing theïbï Clear Allğ button excludes all files in the search.  This is useful, for example, if you wish to only search files in the current directory.  Simply press theïbï Clear Allğ button, specify "*.*" in the Pattern section, and press theïbï Includeğ button.
´
÷4÷Performing_the_Find÷Performing the Find÷The Open Watcom Browser
îPerforming_the_Find

Once the filter criteria are set, perform the find operation by clicking on theïbï OKğ button on theïbï Findğ dialog.  This closes the dialog and performs the search.  The Browser examines the symbols in the current global view and selects the first symbol that matches the search criteria.

To find the next symbol that matches the search criteria, chooseïbï Find Nextğ from theïbï Locateğ menu.
´
÷3÷Find_Selected÷Find Selected÷The Open Watcom Browser
îFind_Selected

ìBrowser, find selectedì Because the symbols displayed in a global view are often many, only a portion of the display is visible in the window at any time.  When you temporarily scroll away from the selected symbol or node, a quick method of locating that symbol is to selectïbï Find Selectedğ from theïbï Locateğ menu.  This will immediately locate and display the currently selected symbol in the global view.
´
÷2÷Configuring_the_Browser÷Configuring the Browser÷The Open Watcom Browser
îConfiguring_the_Browser

ìBrowser, configureì There are several options in the Browser that you can configure:

Òc
Ó Regular expression processing for find and query

Ó Global Symbol Queries

Ó Enumerator styles for detail views of enumeration constants

Ó Member filters for detail views of classes

Ó Auto-arranging of graph views

Ó Line drawing method for graph views

Ó Orientation of graph views

Ó Line styles and colors for inheritance graphs

Ó Line styles and colors for call graphs

Ó Selection of text editor

Ó Automatic saving of options on exit
Ô

This section describes each configuration option.
´
÷3÷Regular_Expressions_for_Find_and_Query÷Regular Expressions for Find and Query÷The Open Watcom Browser
îRegular_Expressions_for_Find_and_Query

ìBrowser, set regular expressions for findì ìBrowser, set regular expressions for queriesì ìregular expressions, in Browserìïbï Regular Expressionsğ in theïbï Optionsğ menu allows you to configure the regular expression used to find a symbol and specify a query.

courier12°c°brow14.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 14.ğïibïğïbï Use the Regular Expressions Options dialog to configure a regular expression for Find and Query.ğ

Chooseïbï Regular Expressionsğ from theïbï Optionsğ menu to open theïbï Regular Expressions Optionsğ dialog.  In theïbï Search String Meaningğ section of the dialog you select the anchoring method used to find a match.

Ê
ÌStarts With
¹ Matches only if the pattern is found at the beginning of the symbol

ÌContains
¹ Matches if it occurs anywhere in the symbol
Ë

In theïbï Regular Expression Charactersğ section of the dialog you select the characters you want the Browser to interpret as meta-characters.

Select the desired characters by clicking once in the corresponding check box.  An X in the check box indicates that the character will be interpreted as a meta-character.  Unchecked characters are matched as standard keyboard characters.  To de-select a character, click again on its check box.

The other buttons on this dialog are:

Ê
ÌSet All
¹ Click on Set All to enable all of the characters.

ÌClear All
¹ Click on Clear All to disable all of the characters.

ÌDefaults
¹ Click on Defaults to discard the current settings in this dialog and replace them with the settings configured at the start of the Browser session.

ÌOK
¹ Click onïbï OKğ to close this dialog.  This changes the configuration for the current Browser session.
Ë
´
÷3÷Global_Symbol_Queries÷Global Symbol Queries÷The Open Watcom Browser
îGlobal_Symbol_Queries

Chooseïbï Queryğ from theïbï Optionsğ menu to open theïbï Queryğ dialog.  Use this dialog to specify the criteria to be used to display symbols in a global view.

courier12°c°brow15.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 15.ğïibïğïbï Use the Query dialog to configure global view symbol queries.ğ
´
÷4÷Query_Pattern÷Query Pattern÷The Open Watcom Browser
îQuery_Pattern

ìBrowser, specifying the query patternì ìquery patternì In theïbï Patternğ field of theïbï Queryğ dialog, enter a pattern for the symbol(s) you wish to display in the global views.  The matching behaviour depends on the switches set in this dialog.

Ê
ÌMatch Case
¹ When enabled, the Browser performs a case sensitive compare when attempting to find a match.

ÌMatch Whole Symbols Only
¹ When enabled, the Browser includes symbols that exactly match the specified pattern.  This switch only applies when theïbï Use Regular Expressionsğ switch is disabled.

ÌUse Regular Expressions
¹ When enabled, the Browser interprets the pattern specified as a regular expression.  Theïbï Editğ button is enables and, when pressed, displays theïbï Regular Expression Optionsğ dialog.  This dialog allows you to specify the regular expression.
Ë

Dropping the combo box of theïbï Patternğ field displays a list of previous patterns entered.  You can select a pattern from this list instead of entering a new one.
´
÷4÷Query_Filters÷Query Filters÷The Open Watcom Browser
îQuery_Filters

ìquery filtersì ìBrowser, query filtersì Clicking on theïbï Filtersğ button on theïbï Queryğ dialog opens theïbï Query Filtersğ dialog.  This dialog allows you to specify the characteristics of the symbol(s) to be displayed in the global views.

In theïbï Symbol Typeğ section of the dialog click on the symbol types you want to view.  To display symbols of all types, click on theïbï Allğ button.

In theïbï Symbol Scopeğ section, you can specify the scope of the symbols to be displayed.

Ê
ÌMember of Class:
¹ Specify the class that the symbol must be a member of in order to be displayed.

ÌLocal Symbols of Function:
¹ Specify the function that the symbol must be local to in order to be displayed.
Ë

In theïbï Symbol Attributesğ section, you can specify the attributes of the symbols to be displayed.  Selectingïbï Artificialğ will cause compiler-generated symbols to be displayed.  Selectingïbï Anonymousğ will cause unnamed types to be displayed.  Unnamed types will be displayed as square brackets enclosing the symbols whose type they define.  Selectingïbï Declared Onlyğ will cause only symbols that have been defined to be displayed.  For example, a function prototype for an unreferenced function will not be displayed whenïbï Declared Onlyğ is selected.

helv12ïbïQuery File Filtersğhelv10
Theïbï Source Filesğ button, when pressed, displays theïbï Source Filesğ dialog that allows you to specify the files that a symbol must be defined in in order to be displayed.  This dialog lists all source files that make up the database file.  Each source file in the list has a check box.  An X in this box indicates that all symbols defined in the source file will be displayed.  Click in the check box of the source file you want to disable.  This removes the X.  The blank box indicates that any symbols defined in the source file will not be displayed.  Alternatively, use the up and down arrow keys to select the source file you wish to disable.  Press the space bar to disable the currently selected source file.  To re-enable the source file, click in the check box again or press the space bar.  This places an X in the box again.

Theïbï Patternğ section of the dialog, allows you to specify wild card directory specifications for files.  Any symbols defined in these files will be displayed in the global views.  For example, specifyingcourier12 d:\watcom\h\*.hhelv10 and pressing theïbï Excludeğ button, will prevent any symbol defined in any file in thecourier12 d:\watcom\hhelv10 directory with extension ".h" from being displayed.  To display symbols from these files again, specify the same pattern and press theïbï Includeğ button.

Pressing theïbï Set Allğ button displays all symbols in all files.

Pressing theïbï Clear Allğ button causes no symbols to be displayed.  This is useful, for example, if you wish to only display symbols defined in files in the current directory.  Simply press theïbï Clear Allğ button, specifycourier12 *.*helv10 in the Pattern section, and press theïbï Includeğ button.
´
÷3÷Enumerator_Styles÷Enumerator Styles÷The Open Watcom Browser
îEnumerator_Styles

ìenumeration stylesì ìBrowser, enumeration stylesì Selectingïbï Enumeration Stylesğ from theïbï Optionsğ menu displays theïbï Enumeration Stylesğ dialog.  This dialog allows you to specify the format for displaying enumeration constants in the detail views for enumeration constants.

courier12°c°brow16.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 16.ğïibïğïbï Specify enumerator styles for detail views of enumerator constants using the Enumeration Styles dialog.ğ
´
÷3÷Member_Filters_for_Classes÷Member Filters for Classes÷The Open Watcom Browser
îMember_Filters_for_Classes

ìBrowser, member filters for class detailsì ìclass details, member filtersìïbï Member Filtersğ in theïbï Optionsğ menu allows you to specify the members you want to appear in the detail view of a class.  For example, you may not want the detail class to contain private members.  Alternatively, you may only wish to see function members and not data members.

Ê
Ì(1)
¹ïbï Chooseğïbï Member Filtersğ from theïbï Optionsğ menu.

This opens theïbï Member Filtersğ dialog where you specify the information you want to appear in detail views for classes.

courier12°c°brow17.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 17.ğïibïğïbï Use the Member Filters dialog to select the information to appear in detail views for classes.ğ

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose the inheritance levelğ from theïbï Inherited Membersğ section of the dialog.

The options are:

Ê
ÌNone
¹ Do not show inherited members.

ÌVisible
¹ Show the local members of a class and visible members of inherited classes.

ÌAll
¹ Show all local and inherited members of a class.
Ë

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Click on the check boxesğ in theïbï Access Levelğ section of the dialog to select the desired access levels.

The options are Public, Protected, and Private.  Only members with the selected attributes will appear in the detail view for a class.

Ì(4)
¹ïbï Select the desired membersğ in theïbï Membersğ section of the dialog.

Show data members in a class by enabling theïbï variablesğ check box.  When theïbï variablesğ check box is enabled, you may also enable or disable static data members by clicking on theïbï staticğ check box.

Show function members in a class by enabling theïbï functionsğ check box.  When theïbï functionsğ check box is enabled, you may also enable or disable static and virtual function members by clicking on theïbï staticğ andïbï virtualğ check boxes.

Ì(5)
¹ïbï Click on OKğ to accept the member filter query.

This closes the dialog and returns you to the active window.
Ë

Ê
ÌNote:
¹ The Default button on the Member Filter dialog resets the query to the default settings.
Ë
´
÷3÷AutoMarranging_of_Graph_Views÷Auto-arranging of Graph Views÷The Open Watcom Browser
îAutoMarranging_of_Graph_Views

ìBrowser, auto-arranging the graph viewì ìgraph views, auto-arrangingì When collapsing a graph view, nodes that become hidden are replaced by gaps in the graph.ïbï  Arrange Graphğ in theïbï Treeğ menu compacts the graph view to remove the spaces left vacant by hidden nodes.

When enabled,ïbï Graph Auto-arrangeğ of theïbï Optionsğ menu causes the Browser to automatically compact the graph view each time you perform a collapse operation.  To enable the automatic compaction of the graph view after a collapse operation, selectïbï Graph Auto-arrangeğ from theïbï Optionsğ menu.  A check mark appears beside the menu item when it is enabled.  Select the menu item again to disable it.
´
÷3÷Line_Drawing_Method_for_Graph_Views÷Line Drawing Method for Graph Views÷The Open Watcom Browser
îLine_Drawing_Method_for_Graph_Views

ïbïGraph Square Linesğ in theïbï Optionsğ menu allows you to select the type of line that connects the nodes in a graph view.  The default is to connect nodes of the graph using diagonal lines.  Choosingïbï Graph Square Linesğ from theïbï Optionsğ menu causes nodes to be connected using square lines (combinations of vertical and horizontal lines).  A check mark beside the menu item indicates this method of drawing lines is enabled.  To disable this option, select it again.

courier12°c°brow18.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 18.ğïibïğïbï The Graph Square Lines option changes the connecting lines from diagonal to square.ğ
´
÷3÷Changing_the_Graph_Orientation÷Changing the Graph Orientation÷The Open Watcom Browser
îChanging_the_Graph_Orientation

ìBrowser, change graph orientationì In theïbï Optionsğ menu you can select whether the trees on the graph view grow horizontally or vertically.  A graph view that grows horizontally is one where the root node is at the left and the leaf nodes at the right.  A graph view that grows vertically is one where the root node is at the top and the leaf nodes at the bottom.  By default, graphs grow vertically.

Selectïbï Graph Horizontalğ from theïbï Optionsğ menu to change the graph orientation to horizontal.  Selecting this option changes the menu item name toïbï Graph Horizontalğ in theïbï Optionsğ menu.  Select this menu item to change the graph orientation back to vertical.

courier12°c°brow19.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 19.ğïibïğïbï The Graph Horizontal option displays the graph with the root node at the left.ğ
´
÷3÷Defining_Graph_View_Legends÷Defining Graph View Legends÷The Open Watcom Browser
îDefining_Graph_View_Legends

ìgraph view legends, in Browserì ìinheritance legend menu itemì ìcall legend menu itemì ìBrowser, defining graph view legendsì Theïbï Inheritance Legendğ andïbï Call Legendğ menu items in theïbï Optionsğ menu allow you to change the colors, line styles, and node styles used in the graph views.  Changing the styles updates all open graph views in the current session, as well as any new ones you open.  Saving the session configuration to an option file saves changes made to the colors and the lines and node styles.

Theïbï Inheritance Legendğ andïbï Call Legendğ dialogs are designed differently, but the procedures for changing the graph styles are identical.

To change the line and node styles for inheritance graphs, chooseïbï Inheritance Legendğ from theïbï Optionsğ menu.  Theïbï Inheritance Legendğ dialog appears.

To change the line and node styles for call graphs, chooseïbï Call Legendğ from theïbï Optionsğ menu.  Theïbï Call Legendğ dialog appears.

Ê
Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the line or node styleğ or use the up, down, right and left arrow keys to select the line or node style you want to change.

A box appears around the selected line.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Click on the Modify button.ğ

This opens theïbï Draw Styleğ dialog for the selected line or node style.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Click once on the desired line or node styleğ

or

Ì 
¹ïbï use the up and down arrow keysğ to select the desired line or node style.

The sample appearing in theïbï Exampleğ field at the top of the dialog now reflects the currently selected line or node style.

Ì(4)
¹ïbï Click once on the desired color or use the up and downğ arrow keys to select the desired color.

The sample appearing in theïbï Exampleğ field at the top of the dialog now reflects the currently selected color.

Ì(5)
¹ïbï Click on OKğ to accept the new style and color setting.

Theïbï Draw Styleğ dialog closes, returning you to theïbï Inheritance Legendğ orïbï Call Legendğ dialog.  Select another line or node style to change and repeat this procedure.

Ì(6)
¹ïbï Click on OK in theğïbï Inheritance Legendğ orïbï Call Legendğ dialog when you have changed all desired colors and styles for lines and nodes.

Clickingïbï OKğ closes the dialog and updates all open inheritance or call graph views with the selected colors and line and node styles.
Ë
´
÷3÷Selecting_a_Text_Editor÷Selecting a Text Editor÷The Open Watcom Browser
îSelecting_a_Text_Editor

ìBrowser, selecting a text editorì ìset text editorì You can use your own favourite text editor from within the Browser.

Ê
ÌTo select your own text editor:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose Set Text Editorğ from the Options menu.

The Set Text Editor dialog appears.  You can enter the name of the text editor in the first field.  You must also indicate whether the text editor is an executable file or a Dynamic Link Library (DLL).  If the editor is an executable file (rather than a DLL), then you can enter an argument line in the second field.  The argument line will be supplied to the editor whenever it is started by the Browser.  The argument line can include any of three different macros which will be filled in by the Browser.  The macros are:

Ê
Ì%f
¹ The name of the file to be edited.

Ì%r
¹ The row in the file at which to position the cursor.  If the editor is invoked from a diagnostic message which contains a line number then the row value is extracted from the message; otherwise the row value is 1.

Ì%c
¹ The column in the file at which to position the cursor.  If the editor is invoked from a diagnostic message which contains a column number then the column value is extracted from the message; otherwise the column value is 1.
Ë

For example, if the editor argument line that you specified was:

courier12      helv10
courier12     file='%f' row='%r' col='%c'helv10

and you double click on an error message in the Log window that names the filecourier12 foobar.chelv10 with an error at line 215 and column 31, then the argument line that is passed to your editor is:

courier12      helv10
courier12     file='foobar.c' row='215' col='31'helv10

This flexibility allows you to specify the name of the file to edit and the row and/or column at which to position the text cursor.  If no row or column is available, then the Browser will supply the value of 1 as a default.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Select OKğ when you wish to confirm the selection of a new editor.

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Select Cancelğ when you wish to cancel the selection of a new editor.

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Select Defaultğ when you wish to restore the default editor selection and then select OK or Cancel.
Ë
´
÷3÷Automatically_Saving_Options_on_Exit÷Automatically Saving Options on Exit÷The Open Watcom Browser
îAutomatically_Saving_Options_on_Exit

ìBrowser, saving options on exitìïbï Save Options on Exitğ in theïbï Optionsğ menu instructs the Browser to automatically save the current options to an options file.  To enable the automatic saving of options, selectïbï Save Options on Exitğ from theïbï Optionsğ menu.  A check mark beside the menu item indicates that it is enabled.  To disable this option, select it again.

See the section entitled Saving_OptionsSaving Options for more information on saving options to a file.
´
÷2÷Loading_Options÷Loading Options÷The Open Watcom Browser
îLoading_Options

ìBrowser, loading optionsì ìoptions, loadingì Use theïbï Load Optionsğ menu to load an option file into your Browser session.

Chooseïbï Load Optionsğ from theïbï File menu.ğ  Theïbï Load Options Fileğ dialog appears where you select the option file you want to load for the current Browser session.
´
÷2÷Saving_Options÷Saving Options÷The Open Watcom Browser
îSaving_Options

ìBrowser, saving optionsì There are three ways to save the current options to an option file:

Òc
Ó Save Options

Ó Save Options As

Ó Save Options on Exit
Ô

An asterisk beside the option file name in the caption bar indicates that changes were made to the options during the Browser session and should be saved.  This section describes each method for saving options to a file.

Ê
ÌNote:
¹ If you have made option changes during the Browser session and you chooseïbï Exitğ from theïbï Fileğ menu without first saving the options, the Browser prompts you to save the options to a file.  Refer to the section entitled Quitting_the_BrowserQuitting the Browser for more information.
Ë

Ê
ÌSave Options
¹ Selectingïbï Save Optionsğ from theïbï Fileğ menu saves the updated options information to the current options file.  To save options in this way, an options file must be specified in the caption bar.  ìBrowser, save optionsì

ÌSave Options As
¹ïbï Save Options Asğ in theïbï Fileğ menu opens theïbï Save Options Asğ dialog.  Specify the filename of the option file to which you want to save the current options.  Click onïbï OKğ to close the dialog and save the options.

ÌSave Options on Exit
¹ïbï Save Options on Exitğ in theïbï Optionsğ menu allows you to specify that the Browser is to automatically save the current options to an options file, if one exists.  When enabled, a check mark appears beside this menu item.

When enabled and an option file exists, the Browser saves the changes without prompting when you close the session.

When enabled and no option file exists, the Browser displays a message box prompting you to save the new options.  Chooseïbï Noğ in this box to close the Browser session without saving.  Chooseïbï Yesğ in this box to display aïbï Save Asğ dialog where you specify the option file to which you want to save the new options.
Ë
´
÷2÷Setting_Source_Search_Paths÷Setting Source Search Paths÷The Open Watcom Browser
îSetting_Source_Search_Paths

ìpath optionì ìBrowser, path optionì ìsource search pathì ìsearch pathì You can specify a command line option to the Browser that allows you to specify alternate source file search paths.  This option is useful when a database file is created on a system different from the one that is used to browse the application source code.  The syntax of the command line option is:

courier12      helv10
courier12     path path_spec1;path_spec2;...helv10

Since the database files record explicit paths to source files, it is likely that the path will no longer be valid once the software is moved to another system.

ìpath searchì When the Browser cannot locate the specified file using its explicit path, it will search the paths listed in the path option.
